#
# lucid-ddos pre-lab quiz (Labtainer quiz format, True/False questions; see the Labtainer lab designer
# guide, section 7). Take it before the lab:
#   on the Labtainer VM:        quiz -l lucid-ddos -q prelab     (self-check, answers are not saved)
#   in the server terminal:     python3 quiz.py prelab           (answers go to the instructor)
# server/quiz/prelab.quiz must stay identical to this file (tools/static_checks.py checks it).
#
0, Preface,
"Pre-lab quiz: DDoS attacks, flow-based detection and the LUCID CNN. Answer T (true) or F (false). \
The quiz checks whether you are ready for the lab; the theory is in Sections 1-3 of the manual."
1, TrueFalse, "In a DDoS attack the traffic comes from many hosts at the same time, so blocking a single address is rarely enough.", T,
"The attack is distributed: a botnet sends the traffic from many addresses.",
"A DDoS attack is distributed: a botnet sends the traffic from many addresses, so blocking one address is rarely enough."
2, TrueFalse, "A SYN flood is an application-layer attack made of valid HTTP requests.", F,
"A SYN flood is a protocol attack: it opens TCP connections and never completes the handshake.",
"A SYN flood is a protocol attack that fills the table of half-open TCP connections. The HTTP flood is the application-layer attack."
3, TrueFalse, "LUCID waits until a flow has ended before it classifies the flow.", F,
"LUCID decides window by window (t = 100 s) on the part of each flow it has seen so far.",
"An online detector cannot wait for a flow to end. LUCID collects the traffic in time windows of t = 100 s and decides on the part of each flow seen in the window."
4, TrueFalse, "A request and its answer belong to the same (bidirectional) flow.", T,
"Packets are grouped by their 5-tuple in both directions, so a request and its answer are seen together.",
"Flows are bidirectional: both directions of a conversation are grouped by the same 5-tuple."
5, TrueFalse, "The source and destination IP addresses are among the 11 LUCID packet features.", F,
"The 5-tuple only groups packets into flows; it is not a model input.",
"LUCID leaves addresses and ports out on purpose: the 5-tuple only groups packets into flows."
6, TrueFalse, "A model that memorises the attacker's IP address can score perfectly on the test split and still fail when the attacker uses another address.", T,
"This is shortcut learning, which Experiment 2 makes visible.",
"In a recorded dataset the attacker always uses the same address, so memorising it works on the test split but fails on a new address (shortcut learning, Experiment 2)."
7, TrueFalse, "Every sample is an n x f matrix; a flow with fewer than n packets is padded with rows of zeros.", T,
"Longer flows are cut after n packets; shorter ones are padded with zeros.",
"The CNN needs a fixed input size: shorter flows are padded with rows of zeros and longer ones are cut after n packets."
8, TrueFalse, "The minimum and maximum of the global normalization are measured on the test split, so that the test data uses the full 0-1 range.", F,
"The scaling is always fitted on the training split only.",
"The scaling is fitted on the training split only; fitting it on the test data would leak information about the test set into training."
9, TrueFalse, "The convolution kernel of the LUCID CNN is as wide as the sample matrix, so it slides only along the packet axis.", T,
"Each kernel covers 3 consecutive packets and all f features.",
"The kernel is 3 x f: it covers all features and slides only along the packets, so the order of the packets matters."
10, TrueFalse, "A threshold detector that counts the packets of every source address reliably detects a botnet that spreads its attack over many addresses.", F,
"Spread over many addresses, every single source stays under the per-source limit.",
"A distributed attack keeps every source under the per-source limit, so the threshold detector misses it (Experiment 4)."
